###Abstract### 
    When it comes to complex machine learning models, commonly referred to as black boxes, understanding the underlying decision making process is crucial for domains such as 
    healthcare and financial services, and also when it is used in connection with safety critical systems such as autonomous vehicles. As such interest in explainable artificial 
    intelligence (xAI) tools and techniques has increased in recent years. However, the effectiveness of existing xAI frameworks, especially concerning algorithms that work with 
    data as opposed to images, is still an open research question. In order to address this gap, in this paper we examine the effectiveness of the Local Interpretable Model-Agnostic 
    Explanations (LIME) xAI framework, one of the most popular model agnostic frameworks found in the literature, with a specific focus on its performance in terms of making tabular 
    models more interpretable. In particular, we apply several state of the art machine learning algorithms on a tabular dataset, and demonstrate how LIME can be used to supplement 
    conventional performance assessment methods. In addition, we evaluate the understandability of the output produced by LIME both via a usability study, involving participants 
    who are not familiar with LIME, and its overall usability via an assessment framework, which is derived from the International Organisation for Standardisation 9241-11:1998 standard.

###AI Summary### 
    The paper investigates how useful LIME is for explaining machine-learning models that work with tabular data. The authors argue that traditional measures such as accuracy, precision 
    or ROC curves tell us whether a model performs well, but they do not explain why the model made a particular decision. They therefore apply LIME to several machine-learning models 
    and examine whether its local explanations provide additional insight into the models' predictions. This is especially relevant because, according to the authors, LIME's tabular 
    explainer had received considerably less evaluation than its image-based use. 

    The results suggest that LIME can be useful for understanding individual predictions and can reveal information that ordinary performance metrics cannot. However, the authors also 
    identify limitations. LIME's explanations are local, meaning they explain one prediction at a time and do not necessarily describe the behaviour of the whole model. Their usability 
    study also showed that people who were unfamiliar with LIME could find the explanations confusing at first, particularly when trying to understand how the local indicators relate to 
    the prediction. Once users became familiar with the explanations, they found them considerably more useful.

    For your research, this paper is important because it deals directly with LIME on tabular data. However, unlike Ross et al., it does not really investigate computational cost scaling. 
    In fact, the authors describe LIME's processing time as short in their particular experiments. This does not mean LIME has no scaling problem; it simply means their experiment was not 
    designed to test what happens as the number of features, samples or explanation requests increases.

###Paper Value### 
    This paper is highly relevant to our research because it specifically evaluates LIME on tabular machine-learning models. It demonstrates that LIME can provide useful explanations that 
    supplement conventional model-performance metrics and can help users understand why individual predictions are made. This gives us strong support for using LIME as the explainability 
    method in a study involving tabular data. 

    However, the paper does not directly investigate how LIME's computational cost scales as the number of features increases. The authors mention that LIME had relatively short processing 
    times in their experiments, but they do not systematically vary the number of features or measure how explanation time changes with dimensionality. Therefore, this paper is useful for 
    establishing LIME's strengths and limitations on tabular data, while our research could extend this by specifically investigating whether those explanations become increasingly expensive 
    as tabular datasets become more complex.

###Proof Paper is Valid###
    This paper is a legitimate academic research paper by Jürgen Dieber and Sabrina Kirrane and is indexed through both WU Vienna University of Economics and Business and DBLP. However, it is 
    listed as a working paper/preprint, so it does not have the same publication status as a peer-reviewed conference paper such as the Ross et al. paper. It is still useful, especially because 
    it directly studies LIME, but that distinction is worth mentioning.
